eedc6e6a-b32b-43fa-a12a-e21d2edd75c9	A **circuit breaker** is a design pattern used in distributed systems to prevent failures from propagating throughout the system. It acts as a safety mechanism to avoid faulty or failed components from affecting the rest of the system.\n\n### Key Concepts:\n- **Open State**: The circuit is open, and the system is not protected. It means the system is actively calling services, and if a failure occurs, it will not propagate the failure to other parts of the system.\n- **Half-Open State**: The circuit is open, but the system is monitoring the service. If a failure is detected, it remains open to prevent further failures from propagating.\n- **Closed State**: The circuit is closed, and the system is protected. It means the system is not calling the service, and any calls will be routed to a fallback mechanism.\n- **Trip State**: The circuit is tripped due to a failure, and the system is not calling the service.\n- **Reset State**: The circuit is reset, and the system is again able to call the service.\n\n### Purpose:\n- Prevents cascading failures by isolating faulty or failed components.\n- Allows the system to continue operating even if some components are failing.\n- Provides a fallback mechanism when the circuit is open.\n\n### Example in Java:\n```java\npublic class CircuitBreaker {\n    private boolean isOpen = false;\n    private int callCount = 0;\n    private int failureCount = 0;\n    private int tripped = 0;\n\n    public boolean call() {\n        if (isOpen) {\n            // If circuit is open, return false to indicate failure\n            return false;\n        }\n\n        // If circuit is closed, call the service\n        callCount++;\n        if (callCount >= 5) {\n            failureCount++;\n            if (failureCount >= 3) {\n                tripped = 1;\n                isOpen = true;\n                return false;\n            }\n        }\n\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    public boolean reset() {\n        if (tripped == 1) {\n            tripped = 0;\n            isOpen = false;\n            callCount = 0;\n            failureCount = 0;\n            return true;\n        }\n        return false;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Use Cases:\n- In microservices architectures to prevent a single failure from crashing the entire system.\n- In API gateways to handle failures gracefully.\n- In distributed systems to isolate failures and allow the system to continue operating.	assistant	2026-09-03 19:41:45.419198	2e78f3de-261d-46fa-aa09-74700a404a8f
